# `module::Field::simulate`

[Back to `module::Field`](../Field.md)

Simulates paths and stores them in `field.paths`.

## Signature

```cpp
void module::Field::simulate(pluss::Config cfg = {});
void module::Field::simulate(const module::Model &candidate, pluss::Config cfg = {});
```

## What It Does

The same as [`simulate_paths`](simulate_paths.md) but the result is assigned to the field's `paths` member, so several payoffs, strikes and maturities can be read from the same simulated future, and a later `simulate` replaces them. Use [`clear_paths`](clear_paths.md) to release the memory.

## Parameters

`const module::Model &candidate`
: Another model to simulate.

`pluss::Config cfg`
: Device.

## Returns

Nothing.

## Errors

- As for `simulate_paths`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Simulate and keep. The stored paths serve two strikes: an Asian-style average and a terminal payoff.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::MonteCarlo, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};
  field.controls.mc.paths = 2000;
  field.controls.mc.steps = 50;
  field.controls.mc.seed = 7;

  field.simulate();
  const Series terminal = field.paths.asset.select(1, 50);
  const Series average = field.paths.asset.mean(1);
  out.writeln("E[max(S_T - 100, 0)] =", (terminal - 100.0).relu().mean().item<double>(), ", E[max(avg - 100, 0)] =", (average - 100.0).relu().mean().item<double>());
}
```

### Example 2

Storing the paths of a candidate.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(0));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::MonteCarlo, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};
  field.controls.mc.paths = 2000;
  field.controls.mc.steps = 50;
  field.controls.mc.seed = 7;

  field.simulate(fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.9, -0.7, 0.04}));
  out.writeln("stored shape:", field.paths.asset.sizes());
}
```

### Example 3

A second `simulate` replaces the first.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::MonteCarlo, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};
  field.controls.mc.paths = 2000;
  field.controls.mc.steps = 50;
  field.controls.mc.seed = 7;

  field.simulate();
  const double first = field.paths.asset.select(1, 50).mean().item<double>();
  field.controls.mc.seed = 99;
  field.simulate();
  out.writeln("mean S_T, seed 7 :", first, ", seed 99 :", field.paths.asset.select(1, 50).mean().item<double>());
}
```

